<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Calculating expected net return in a charity raffle</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A community group in Kakamega is organizing a charity raffle to raise funds for a new library. They sell exactly <b>{@total_tickets@}</b> tickets at a cost of <b>KES {@ticket_cost@}</b> each.</p>

<p>There is only one winning ticket, and the grand prize is <b>KES {@prize_amount@}</b>. All other tickets win nothing.</p>

<p><b>a) </b> If you buy exactly one ticket, what is the probability that you win the grand prize?</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as a fully simplified fraction).</i></p>
<p>\(P(\text{Win}) =\) [[input:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> Calculate the <i>expected gross payout</i> (the average mathematical value of the winnings) for a single ticket, before subtracting the cost of buying the ticket.</p>
<p>\(\text{Expected Gross Payout} =\) [[input:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>c) </b> Calculate the <b>expected net return</b> (profit or loss) of purchasing one ticket. If it is an expected loss, be sure to include a negative sign (\(-\)).</p>
<p>\(\text{Expected Net Return} =\) [[input:ans3]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part a: Probability of Winning</b><br>
Since there is only 1 winning ticket out of {@total_tickets@} total tickets sold, the probability is:<br>
\( \displaystyle P(\text{Win}) = \frac{1}{{@total_tickets@}} = {@ta1@} \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: Expected Gross Payout</b><br>
The expected gross payout is the probability of winning multiplied by the prize amount.<br>
\( \displaystyle \text{Expected Gross Payout} = P(\text{Win}) \times \text{Prize Amount} \)<br>
\( \displaystyle \text{Expected Gross Payout} = \frac{1}{{@total_tickets@}} \times {@prize_amount@} = {@ta2@} \)<br>
<span style="font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ \text{Expected Gross Payout} = \text{KES } {@ta2@} } \)</span></p>

<p><b>Part c: Expected Net Return</b><br>
The net return accounts for the initial cost of purchasing the ticket. You must subtract the ticket cost from the expected gross payout.<br>
\( \displaystyle \text{Expected Net Return} = \text{Expected Gross Payout} - \text{Ticket Cost} \)<br>
\( \displaystyle \text{Expected Net Return} = {@ta2@} - {@ticket_cost@} = {@ta3@} \)<br>
<span style="font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ \text{Expected Net Return} = \text{KES } {@ta3@} } \)</span></p>

<p><i>Note: Because the expected net return is negative, buying a raffle ticket is a mathematical loss on average, which makes sense because the goal of the raffle is for the charity to make a profit!</i></p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
